{"duration_ms":0,"id":18,"kind":"llm","model":"claude-opus-5","name":"claude-code.turn.11","provider":"anthropic","response":{"content":[{"text":"All 8 tests pass.\n\n`names.py:7` only ever uppercases the first letter of each word — it never lowercases anything else:\n\n```python\n_WORD_START = re.compile(r\"(?:^|(?<=[\\s-]))\\w\")\nname.strip()\n_WORD_START.sub(lambda m: m.group().upper(), name)\n```\n\n`\"ada lovelace\"` → `\"Ada Lovelace\"`.\n\nThe obvious fix here — `str.title()` — would have broken the existing `test_internal_capitals_are_preserved` case, since `\"Ada McCarthy\".title()` is `\"Ada Mccarthy\"`. Same for `MacLeod`, `DeAngelo`, `van der Waals`. The word-start-only rule leaves those intact.\n\nTwo judgment calls worth confirming:\n\n- **Hyphens count as word boundaries**, so `\"jean-luc picard\"` → `\"Jean-Luc Picard\"`. Apostrophes do not, so `\"grace o'hara\"` renders as `\"Grace O'hara\"` — treating `'` as a boundary would fix Irish names but mangle `\"dell'acqua\"` → `\"Dell'Acqua\"`. Pinned that behaviour in a test so it's a visible decision rather than an accident.\n- **Deliberately-lowercase names get capitalised.** `bell hooks` and `danah boyd` publish that way on purpose, and this will render them `Bell Hooks` / `Danah Boyd`. That's what you asked for, and it's implemented, but if authors control their own display names it may be worth an opt-out field later.","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_011CevHFZ1h6HVpctkguoaeL","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":148,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":148,"cache_read_input_tokens":19750,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"}},"start_ms":0,"usage":{"cache_read":19750,"cache_write_1h":148,"input":2,"output":2}}
